      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the given problem, we start with the equation:\n\n\\[ ab + bc + cd + da = 30 \\]\n\nand the condition:\n\n\\[ b + d = 5. \\]\n\nFirst, we can factor the left-hand side of the equation:\n\n\\[ ab + bc + cd + da = (a+c)b + (a+d)c. \\]\n\nSo the equation becomes:\n\n\\[ (a+c)b + (a+d)c = 30. \\]\n\nWe can rewrite this as:\n\n\\[ (a+c)b + (a+d)c = (a+c)b + c(a+d) = (a+c)b + c(5 - b) = (a+c)b + 5c - bc = ab + bc + cd + da. \\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[ (a+c)b + (a+d)c = 30. \\]\n\nSince \\( b + d = 5 \\), we can substitute \\( d = 5 - b \\) into the equation:\n\n\\[ (a+c)b + (a+(5-b))c = 30, \\]\n\\[ (a+c)b + (a+5-b)c = 30, \\]\n\\[ (a+c)b + (a+c)c = 30, \\]\n\\[ (a+c)(b+c) = 30. \\]\n\nNow, we need to find the values of \\( a \\) and \\( c \\) that satisfy this equation. Let's assume \\( a + c = x \\), then the equation becomes:\n\n\\[ x(b+c) = 30. \\]\n\nWe need to find integer solutions for \\( a \\) and \\( c \\) such that \\( a + c = x \\) and \\( b + d = 5 \\). Let's test some possible values for \\( x \\).\n\nIf \\( x = 6 \\), then:\n\n\\[ 6(b+c) = 30, \\]\n\\[ b+c = 5. \\]\n\nThis means \\( a + c = 6 \\) and \\( b + d = 5 \\) is consistent. So, we can choose \\( a = 3 \\) and \\( c = 3 \\) (or any other pair that adds up to 6). Let's verify:\n\nIf \\( a = 3 \\) and \\( c = 3 \\), then:\n\n\\[ ab + bc + cd + da = 3b + 3c + 3d + 3a = 3(b+c) + 3d + 3a = 3 \\cdot 5 + 3d + 3 \\cdot 3 = 15 + 3d + 9 = 30. \\]\n\nThis satisfies the given equation. Therefore, the value of \\( a + c \\) is:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{6}. \\]",
